Package: pydebuprep
Architecture: all
Depends: ${python3:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, python3-yaml
Recommends: debsums, needrestart
Suggests: efibootmgr, dkms
Description: health report suite for Debian and Ubuntu after a release upgrade
 pydebuprep inspects a Debian or Ubuntu system after a release upgrade
 (for example Debian 12 to 13, or Ubuntu 22.04 to 24.04) and reports on
 package state and APT sources, configuration file conflicts, the kernel
 and bootloader, systemd units, timers and cron jobs, logs, time
 synchronisation, network and firewall, AppArmor and SSH, PostgreSQL,
 MariaDB, postfix, BIND, Docker and Python virtualenvs, mounts and disk
 space.
 .
 It never changes system or application settings, or the state of runtime
 files such as apt's and dpkg's; scratch files go under /tmp or /var/tmp and
 are removed again. Every finding comes with the commands to fix it. An
 optional snapshot taken before the upgrade lets the report show what
 changed. Reports are available as text, JSON, YAML, HTML, Markdown, Jira and
 Confluence markup, with exit codes suited to automation such as Ansible.
 .
 The separate pydebuprep-cleanup command, run on purpose, purges what an
 upgrade leaves behind: old kernels, their modules and headers, leftover
 kernel directories and the configuration of removed packages. It only shows
 its plan unless asked to act, backs up configuration files first, and logs
 every action.
